WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.752
A city is not a museum.

00:00:02.752 --> 00:00:04.129
A city is really kind of

00:00:04.129 --> 00:00:06.214
almost a living, 
breathing organism.

00:00:06.214 --> 00:00:07.716
Austin’s the greatest 
city on earth.

00:00:07.716 --> 00:00:09.300
Unfortunately, the secret is out.

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:13.304
Our existential challenge 
is affordability issues.

00:00:13.304 --> 00:00:17.100
The American dream of owning 
a home is harder than ever.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:19.144
It’s especially out of 
reach for Austinites,

00:00:19.144 --> 00:00:21.604
who are facing every 
issue imaginable.

00:00:21.604 --> 00:00:23.440
We traveled to Texas to learn

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:25.108
more about their housing crisis

00:00:25.108 --> 00:00:26.943
and possible solutions.

00:00:36.244 --> 00:00:40.832
Austin population growth has been growing at a very high clip

00:00:40.832 --> 00:00:42.667
for almost 150 years.

00:00:42.667 --> 00:00:44.794
What changed in the 1980s

00:00:44.794 --> 00:00:47.589
is that many different types

00:00:47.589 --> 00:00:50.258
of housing were limited

00:00:50.258 --> 00:00:53.386
or even banned 
to a certain extent.

00:00:53.386 --> 00:00:54.846
Think of a land 
development code

00:00:54.846 --> 00:00:56.389
as having zoning tools.

00:00:56.389 --> 00:00:57.640
So you look at a piece of land.

00:00:57.640 --> 00:00:59.225
You look at the needs 
of a community

00:00:59.225 --> 00:01:00.226
and then you can figure out

00:01:00.226 --> 00:01:02.645
what you can craft 
to fit on this land

00:01:02.645 --> 00:01:03.980
that fits within that zoning tool.

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:05.523
We rewrote our land 
development code

00:01:05.523 --> 00:01:07.817
and there was a big downzoning
for much of Austin.

00:01:14.157 --> 00:01:16.534
And housing supply 
didn’t keep pace.

00:01:16.534 --> 00:01:19.079
The housing crisis has 
just reached a level

00:01:19.079 --> 00:01:22.916
where even upper, middle- 
class folks are struggling

00:01:22.916 --> 00:01:25.919
to find housing in Austin.

00:01:25.919 --> 00:01:28.755
We saw a slow and steady

00:01:28.755 --> 00:01:31.257
appreciation of housing prices,

00:01:31.257 --> 00:01:33.384
really since the Great Recession.

00:01:33.384 --> 00:01:35.345
And then in the last 
couple of years,

00:01:35.345 --> 00:01:38.139
we’ve gone from 6% to

00:01:38.139 --> 00:01:42.018
we’re looking at 20% a year
appreciation in home prices.

00:01:42.018 --> 00:01:45.480
So Austin was a big benefactor
of these pandemic moves.

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:47.607
People who are newly able 
to work remotely

00:01:47.607 --> 00:01:50.443
moved their families and 
their high salaries to Austin.

00:01:50.443 --> 00:01:52.904
These low interest rates 
allowed them to buy

00:01:52.904 --> 00:01:55.031
single-family homes, 
buy property,

00:01:55.031 --> 00:01:56.783
and that has driven up costs

00:01:56.783 --> 00:01:59.119
for a lot of the existing 
Austin residents.

00:01:59.619 --> 00:02:01.329
We’ve got two sets of problems

00:02:01.329 --> 00:02:02.580
in a place like Austin:

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:06.334
low-income people having a hard time affording housing.

00:02:06.334 --> 00:02:08.086
And then there’s another 
set of problems:

00:02:08.086 --> 00:02:11.131
our zoning laws are 
making it too expensive

00:02:11.131 --> 00:02:12.924
to build new housing 
that even

00:02:12.924 --> 00:02:14.551
the middle class could afford.

00:02:16.261 --> 00:02:18.346
So back in 2012,

00:02:18.346 --> 00:02:20.682
in an effort to get ahead 
of the coming wave,

00:02:20.682 --> 00:02:23.685
the city council adopted 
a plan called Imagine Austin

00:02:23.685 --> 00:02:25.979
to recommend a new 
zoning framework.

00:02:25.979 --> 00:02:27.188
In Imagine Austin,

00:02:27.188 --> 00:02:29.232
it calls for a new land development code

00:02:29.232 --> 00:02:31.484
that promotes a compact 
and connected city.

00:02:31.484 --> 00:02:33.945
We had this process, 
we dubbed it Code Next.

00:02:33.945 --> 00:02:36.406
A small group of homeowners then filed suit

00:02:36.406 --> 00:02:39.909
and unfortunately blocked that new code from being passed.

00:02:39.909 --> 00:02:42.620
Code Next is stuck in 
the courts right now;

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:45.206
unclear what’s going to happen.

00:02:45.206 --> 00:02:48.668
You run into the culture 
wars and NIMBYism

00:02:48.668 --> 00:02:51.504
as soon as you try to do that.

00:02:51.504 --> 00:02:54.465
Folks that are in these 
legacy neighborhoods

00:02:54.465 --> 00:02:58.178
that are within a few minutes 
of being downtown

00:02:58.178 --> 00:03:01.055
that are there because 
they like the big yard,

00:03:01.055 --> 00:03:03.266
they like the space 
that they have.

00:03:06.269 --> 00:03:08.646
Part of the problem, 
advocates say,

00:03:08.646 --> 00:03:10.857
are restrictive zoning codes

00:03:10.857 --> 00:03:13.151
that have existed in 
Austin for decades.

00:03:13.151 --> 00:03:15.820
It restricts what kind of dense housing can be built where

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:18.156
and it gives property 
owners a lot of say in

00:03:18.156 --> 00:03:20.617
what kinds of developments

00:03:20.617 --> 00:03:22.160
can come into their neighborhoods.

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:23.745
One of the most problematic

00:03:23.745 --> 00:03:26.873
aspects of Austin’s 
zoning regulations

00:03:26.873 --> 00:03:30.084
are the minimum lot size
for a single-family home.

00:03:30.084 --> 00:03:33.588
We have a minimum 
5,700 square foot

00:03:33.588 --> 00:03:36.966
lot size for somebody to build 
a single-family home.

00:03:36.966 --> 00:03:39.052
That is the biggest

00:03:39.052 --> 00:03:41.221
of any of the major 
cities in Texas.

00:03:41.221 --> 00:03:43.806
So now the housing 
costs have grown

00:03:43.806 --> 00:03:45.642
where we have these 
single-family zoned areas.

00:03:45.642 --> 00:03:47.852
We’re just seeing 
$1 million homes,

00:03:47.852 --> 00:03:50.772
$2 million homes replace 
those former homes

00:03:50.772 --> 00:03:52.023
that were just torn down.

00:03:52.023 --> 00:03:54.025
And so we’re not gaining 
housing inventory,

00:03:54.025 --> 00:03:55.693
but then we’re also watching 
that median home

00:03:55.693 --> 00:03:57.987
value of all those 
neighborhoods skyrocket.

00:03:59.155 --> 00:04:01.574
The East Cesar Chavez neighborhood is located

00:04:01.574 --> 00:04:02.909
on the east side of Austin

00:04:02.909 --> 00:04:04.702
and was historically 
one of the most diverse

00:04:04.702 --> 00:04:06.329
communities in the city.

00:04:06.329 --> 00:04:07.538
But gentrification amid

00:04:07.538 --> 00:04:09.249
the housing crisis has 
pushed older residents

00:04:09.249 --> 00:04:12.043
farther away from downtown.

00:04:12.377 --> 00:04:14.921
In East Austin, we have 
zip code 78702.

00:04:14.921 --> 00:04:17.882
That area is now one of the 
most posh areas in Austin.

00:04:17.882 --> 00:04:19.175
So single family homes

00:04:19.175 --> 00:04:21.719
that not that long ago 
were $40,000

00:04:21.719 --> 00:04:22.762
are now over $1 million.

00:04:22.762 --> 00:04:24.055
Places like East Austin,

00:04:24.055 --> 00:04:26.599
which are predominantly Black
and Hispanic neighborhoods,

00:04:26.599 --> 00:04:28.059
have depressed land values,

00:04:28.059 --> 00:04:29.560
which means it’s easier 
for developers

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:31.145
to come and build there

00:04:31.145 --> 00:04:34.941
versus in communities like 
West Austin and Central Austin,

00:04:34.941 --> 00:04:36.401
which are more 
expensive to build on

00:04:36.401 --> 00:04:37.777
and which have whiter, wealthier

00:04:37.777 --> 00:04:40.363
homeowners who are able 
to deter and delay

00:04:40.363 --> 00:04:41.948
these new construction projects.

00:04:41.948 --> 00:04:44.367
So where building happens
is very much influenced

00:04:44.367 --> 00:04:45.952
by who lives in these neighborhoods

00:04:45.952 --> 00:04:48.371
and what kind of political 
power is exerted there.

00:04:48.371 --> 00:04:51.874
The worst thing that we can do

00:04:51.874 --> 00:04:54.168
for displacement and gentrification

00:04:54.168 --> 00:04:56.087
of our working-class population,

00:04:56.087 --> 00:04:58.423
especially our Black 
and brown population,

00:04:58.423 --> 00:04:59.590
is the same thing

00:04:59.590 --> 00:05:01.551
that we’ve been doing 
for the last 20 years.

00:05:01.551 --> 00:05:03.928
We need to build housing

00:05:03.928 --> 00:05:06.431
so all the people that are coming

00:05:06.431 --> 00:05:08.057
because of the Tesla factory,

00:05:08.057 --> 00:05:09.642
because of the Samsung factory

00:05:09.642 --> 00:05:12.687
that’s going to be built,
have housing that they can buy

00:05:12.687 --> 00:05:15.231
and are not competing 
to buy the same

00:05:15.231 --> 00:05:17.859
old little East Austin house.

00:05:21.362 --> 00:05:23.906
If you’re building 
homes in Austin,

00:05:23.906 --> 00:05:26.909
the best and most profitable thing that you can do

00:05:26.909 --> 00:05:30.538
is buy an old home, 
tear that home down

00:05:30.538 --> 00:05:33.458
and build a huge McMansion
on that same property.

00:05:33.458 --> 00:05:36.753
I want that to change to 
where that same lot

00:05:36.753 --> 00:05:39.297
is going to be redeveloped 
with two homes

00:05:39.297 --> 00:05:41.632
or with three homes 
or with a four-plex.

00:05:41.632 --> 00:05:42.800
What’s really on the table is

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:44.344
what are called transition zones,

00:05:44.344 --> 00:05:45.887
which are areas that are sort of

00:05:45.887 --> 00:05:47.680
between those busy streets

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:50.892
and then those quiet interiors
of the neighborhoods.

00:05:50.892 --> 00:05:54.020
And that’s where really a lot of the controversy is right now.

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:56.731
How big should those 
transition zones be?

00:05:56.731 --> 00:05:59.317
I’ll say my personal preference is

00:05:59.317 --> 00:06:02.695
I’d rather just raise the bar 
across the whole city.

00:06:02.695 --> 00:06:05.281
I think that’s a simpler and 
a fairer way to do it.

00:06:05.281 --> 00:06:08.618
That’s in fact what the city 
of Minneapolis did.

00:06:09.243 --> 00:06:12.038
Citywide zoning reform 
has remained elusive.

00:06:12.038 --> 00:06:13.748
In the meantime, Austin voters

00:06:13.748 --> 00:06:15.458
have approved multiple 
bond measures

00:06:15.458 --> 00:06:17.085
to build affordable housing,

00:06:17.085 --> 00:06:21.381
such as a $350 million bond 
in November 2022.

00:06:21.381 --> 00:06:23.925
Honestly, we probably 
need to add

00:06:23.925 --> 00:06:26.260
a $250,000 housing bond

00:06:26.260 --> 00:06:29.138
every two years 
for the next 20 years

00:06:29.138 --> 00:06:31.391
if we want to get a critical

00:06:31.391 --> 00:06:34.060
mass of public housing, 
of affordable,

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:35.853
publicly subsidized housing,

00:06:35.853 --> 00:06:37.397
that there are enough units

00:06:37.397 --> 00:06:39.524
where we are able to house

00:06:39.524 --> 00:06:41.609
a lot of our working 
poor and folks

00:06:41.609 --> 00:06:42.902
that really need help.

00:06:42.902 --> 00:06:45.655
You know, one of the things 
I’m most proud about

00:06:45.655 --> 00:06:47.782
in the time that I’ve been mayor

00:06:47.782 --> 00:06:51.119
is that our city has taken really

00:06:51.119 --> 00:06:55.164
big steps, big enough 
to actually bring forth

00:06:55.164 --> 00:06:58.000
real transformative 
change in our city.

00:06:58.000 --> 00:06:59.419
But if you don’t deal

00:06:59.419 --> 00:07:01.796
with your really big challenges,

00:07:01.796 --> 00:07:04.424
then they just get bigger.

00:07:07.427 --> 00:07:09.387
I think it’s important 
that we look at zoning,

00:07:09.387 --> 00:07:11.055
not only as not a silver bullet,

00:07:11.055 --> 00:07:12.557
but a double-edged sword.

00:07:12.557 --> 00:07:13.933
Upzoning can actually do

00:07:13.933 --> 00:07:15.935
tremendous harm to 
some communities

00:07:15.935 --> 00:07:18.479
and especially the people who are most vulnerable in the market.

00:07:18.479 --> 00:07:21.190
And upzoning only works to 
achieve our housing goals

00:07:21.190 --> 00:07:24.777
if we’re pairing it with other 
tools and collaborations.

00:07:24.777 --> 00:07:27.029
Solutions like building 
on public land

00:07:27.029 --> 00:07:28.656
at deeply affordable rates.

00:07:28.656 --> 00:07:30.950
I’m a firm believer in 
community land trusts.

00:07:30.950 --> 00:07:33.911
Unfortunately, many of the community-based solutions

00:07:33.911 --> 00:07:36.998
that are truly equitable, 
they’re not moving at scale.

00:07:36.998 --> 00:07:38.124
The most affordable units

00:07:38.124 --> 00:07:39.584
we have are the ones 
we have now

00:07:39.584 --> 00:07:42.170
and it costs us much less 
to protect those

00:07:42.170 --> 00:07:44.672
and to preserve those 
than to build new units.

00:07:44.672 --> 00:07:46.340
It’s not to say we shouldn’t 
build new units.

00:07:46.340 --> 00:07:48.092
We need to build new 
affordable housing,

00:07:48.092 --> 00:07:50.720
but we’re going to have to 
disrupt the market as we do it.

00:07:53.222 --> 00:07:55.975
I think that cities that are 
smaller than Austin

00:07:55.975 --> 00:07:57.852
that think they may be 
on the same

00:07:57.852 --> 00:08:02.190
or a similar trajectory over 
the next 10, 15, 20 years

00:08:02.190 --> 00:08:04.567
should be learning,
going to school on us.

00:08:04.567 --> 00:08:06.777
Goodness knows we 
did not do the things

00:08:06.777 --> 00:08:09.572
10 and 20 years ago 
that we should have.

00:08:09.572 --> 00:08:12.200
I would tell other cities

00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:16.204
that they have to prepare for 
the growth that is coming.

00:08:16.204 --> 00:08:19.499
A lot of times the growth and 
the change is uncomfortable.

00:08:19.499 --> 00:08:23.044
There is sometimes a 
resentment of the newcomers,

00:08:23.044 --> 00:08:26.714
but if you don’t build housing 
for the people

00:08:26.714 --> 00:08:28.382
that are moving to your city,

00:08:28.382 --> 00:08:31.594
you’re going to price out 
your current residents.

